Z||000000||cmyokley||08-11-99||08:28 AM||||Please help, this is a strange one for me.<p>I completely rebuilt my calipers several months ago and everything has been fine till last weekend. When I put the car into the garage on saturday there was a leak in the left rear caliper. I thoutht this was quite funny....NOT. Since I live in a very small town (HOUSTON,TX) I had to order the rebuild kit (if you can believe that) and last night I rebuilt the caliper AGAIN. Well a funny thing happened, it still leaked. I took the caliper back off, leaving connected to the brake line, put a spacer in front of the piston so it would not travel out of the caliper and pressured up the system. It started leaking again and brake fluid was coming out the hole in the center of the piston. The hole also has a small piece of rubber in it (also found in the rebuild kit) called a check valve. Not questing GM here but why would an engineer put a hole in a hydraulic piston and then plug it with a piece of rubber. Has the piston gone bad? Am I not rebuilding the caliper right? Why is there a hole in the piston? and what is the purpose of the so called check valve?<p>Any ideas?
Z||000001||Cooter||08-11-99||08:29 PM||||Advance Auto Parts rebuilt caliper- I had to get 3 of them before I finally got one that didnt leak in the same place. I did not try to rebuild them- next time I will because a complete overhaul kit and pistons is cheaper that one rebuilt caliper that still leaked. I would say just spend the 10 bucks on a new piston and see if that fixes it. <p>See, we do read all these posts, even if we dont respond. Now where was that Fiero on Uranus. .  .
Z||000002||theogre||08-11-99||10:48 PM||||We respond when we have something valid, and hopefully accurate, to say. If you don't get an answer,,,,, we don't bloody know. Next time don't whine in another thread. I know allot of stuff, but I don't know everything. Even collectivly we don't know everything. We sure as heck don't know what some designer at GM/Pontiac was smoking when they came up with some of this stuff.
Z||000003||BN Boomer||08-12-99||12:14 AM||||I think this guy's Fiero accidentally received a set of calipers intended for installation on the Fiero Martian Landing Module. They were intended for use with both a lower specific gravity and lower atmospheric pressue. That is why they can't withstand the rigors of operation on earth...
Z||000004||cmyokley||08-12-99||07:21 AM||||Well now sorry to get your pants in a wad.....I just thought that I could get a valaid response to a seemingly simple brake problem...just thought you guys with all the knowledge wold have an answer....sorry<br>
Z||000005||cmyokley||08-12-99||07:28 AM||||By the way COOTER thanks for the reply:)
Z||000006||Cliff Pennock||08-12-99||10:37 AM||||Ok, before this turns into a flame war...<p>There are a lot of messages people have to wade through, especially when it has been a few days since their last visit. This means that sometimes it can take a few days before you'll get a response. Or sometimes people simply have to look it up in a publication of some sort.<p>I can understand your frustration Cmyokley, seeing that a <b>very</b> off-topic thread gets a lot of response, while your serious question gets none... To draw attention to your message again, the most effective way is to reply to your own message with a small simple message like "Reminder", or "Anyone?" or whatever you can think of. This way, your message will get back in the top of the topic-list and people will notice it again.<p>The fact that nobody responds to your message does not mean you're ignored. People either do not know the answer or haven't read it yet.<p>Anyway, being called a whiner is also not necessary and certainly not how we should greet a newcomer to this forum... It'll only start flame wars and that is why I got so sick and tired of other discussion media...<p>I don't read <b>all</b> threads, and I know that in some of these threads some very heated discussions are going on. But I like to think that the members of this forum can moderate themselves instead of one person having to moderate everyone else.<p>To make a long story short, let's keep the atmosphere in this forum a friendly one...<br>
Z||000007||cmyokley||08-12-99||12:30 PM||||Cliff,<p>I have spent the past two years on a ground up rebuild (not restoration) of an 86GT with my 17 yr. old son. In the past two years I have learned a lot about the Fiero from other web sites and books. Thanks to reading your forum I have been able to rectify some of the little nagging problems that have slowed down the rebuild process....in short this has been a great information source.<p>Thanks.
Z||000008||Dale||08-13-99||12:49 PM||||Cliff,<p>WELL SPOKEN, I am also new to this forum and have run across the same thing.  It takes a little bit of time for people to get to know who you are.  In regaurds to the question and correct me if I am wrong.  I believe the check valve was GM's way of preventing brake seal blowout on hard braking.  Better to lose a little bit of fluid than to lose your brakes totally.<p>I hear you about ordering items, we live on Kauai and have to order everything and wait 2 weeks to get anything.
Z||000009||PaUL||08-13-99||06:01 PM||||hey cmyokley I take your problem seriously. My rear calipers used to have that yellow plug in the center of the piston. When I had the parking brake recall done, they changed the rear pistons to a new design without the plug. The rear brakes havent given me any problems since. Hope this helps.
Z||000010||lowCG||08-14-99||02:23 AM||||My first idea was to suggest a T.I.G. plug weld the hole shut,careful to not over-amp,but I thought I'd wait and see what possible reason there would be to do what they did to those pistons.
Z||000011||cmyokley||08-17-99||03:45 PM||||PaUL,<p>Thanks for the information.<p>Over the past several weeks I have noticed several peaple that are talking about the recalls. Is it possible to force GM to do recalls on cars that are so old. Are they still responsible if the recall was not done?<p>Thanks again.
Z||000012||PaUL||08-17-99||04:43 PM||||When I had the brake recall done, my car was already over 10 years old. All GM dealerships are supposed to do the recalls no matter how old the car is. 
Z||000013||fierowner||08-17-99||11:39 PM||||What is this about a parking brake recall?  I've never heard of it; my brake is broke so i'm interested! by the way; My dad and i are planning a rear brake calipers (both) rebuild...gonna have to do that soon, cause i burned up the rear brakes by leaving my supposedly broken parking brake on from my friends house to my house....they were smokin. Havent told dad yet....whooops.
Z||000014||batboy||08-18-99||07:27 AM||||Fieroowner, you can call your local Pontiac dealership and give them your VIN and they can tell you if the car has any outstanding recalls that haven't been done.<p>Unless you've got experience rebuilding calipers, I'd recommend just buying rebuilt ones, much easier (but a little more expensive). Sounds like at the very least you'll need to turn the rotors (they tend to warp under extreme heat) and put new pads on. 
